WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.460 --> 00:00:06.380
Jean-Luc Picard is back for a new adventure
in Star Trek: Picard, once again boldly going

00:00:06.380 --> 00:00:08.280
where no one has gone before.

00:00:08.280 --> 00:00:13.700
The CBS All-Access show is filled with callbacks
to Star Trek past, including some that might

00:00:13.700 --> 00:00:15.900
elude even the most dedicated of fans.

00:00:16.320 --> 00:00:21.900
The first episode of Star Trek: Picard opens
to the music of Irving Berlin, as Bing Crosby

00:00:21.900 --> 00:00:24.280
croons the lyrics to "Blue Skies."

00:00:24.280 --> 00:00:29.100
It's a curious song choice for a show set
in the distant future, but the nearly century-old

00:00:29.100 --> 00:00:32.860
song holds a surprising amount of significance
for Jean-Luc Picard.

00:00:32.860 --> 00:00:38.120
Early on in Star Trek: Nemesis, the final
film in the Next Generation movie series,

00:00:38.120 --> 00:00:43.239
Lieutenant Commander Data channels the classic
crooners of the past by performing "Blue Skies"

00:00:43.240 --> 00:00:45.900
at the wedding of Will Riker and Deanna Troi.

00:00:45.900 --> 00:00:51.020
Toward the end of the film, before Data is
destroyed sacrificing himself along with the

00:00:51.030 --> 00:00:56.850
Reman ship Scimitar, Data attempts to transfer
his memories to B-4, an earlier prototype

00:00:56.850 --> 00:01:00.340
of the android made by his creator, Dr. Noonian
Soong.

00:01:00.340 --> 00:01:05.400
Unfortunately, B-4 is not nearly as advanced
as Data, and it appears the transfer was a

00:01:05.400 --> 00:01:11.460
failure until B-4 begins softly singing "Blue
Skies" to himself at the end of the movie.

00:01:11.460 --> 00:01:16.200
Picard's reaction in Nemesis indicates that
he suspects there may be some of Data still

00:01:16.210 --> 00:01:21.380
alive after all, a thread that is carried
forward into the first episode of Picard,

00:01:21.380 --> 00:01:25.710
in which the former captain not only visits
with Data in his dreams, but speculates that

00:01:25.710 --> 00:01:28.840
the "essence" of Data now lives on in a new
form.

00:01:29.180 --> 00:01:33.660
Since The Next Generation ended with a poker
game, it's only fitting that Picard should

00:01:33.660 --> 00:01:34.840
begin with one.

00:01:34.840 --> 00:01:39.960
The first episode sees Jean-Luc and Data playing
cards aboard the Enterprise, engaged in the

00:01:39.970 --> 00:01:44.649
same sort of conversation we've seen them
participate in countless times before: analyzing

00:01:44.649 --> 00:01:47.440
yet another of Data's attempts to appear more
human.

00:01:47.440 --> 00:01:52.260
This time, Picard is sizing up Data's humanity
in the form of a poker tell.

00:01:52.260 --> 00:01:57.979
Data is dilating one pupil in order to fool
the Captain into believing he has a good hand,

00:01:57.980 --> 00:02:01.400
while Picard deduces that his real tell is
when he does nothing at all.

00:02:01.400 --> 00:02:06.700
The scene helps viewers feel immediately at
home in the new show, bringing back settings,

00:02:06.710 --> 00:02:11.150
themes, and characters that have already been
familiar for decades, but it also calls back

00:02:11.150 --> 00:02:15.120
to the final episode of The Next Generation,
"All Good Things..."

00:02:15.120 --> 00:02:20.480
After skipping through time and saving all
life in the universe, the final scene finds

00:02:20.480 --> 00:02:23.820
the crew of the Enterprise engaged in their
regular poker game.

00:02:23.820 --> 00:02:28.120
They're surprised when Picard arrives and
asks to join, but welcome him to the table,

00:02:28.120 --> 00:02:32.880
and the series ends with the Captain dealing
the cards while saying, "The sky's the limit."

00:02:32.880 --> 00:02:37.580
In Picard, the poker game makes it clear that
we're picking up where the previous show left

00:02:37.580 --> 00:02:42.440
off, and setting it to "Blue Skies" could
even be calling back to Picard's final line.

00:02:42.440 --> 00:02:47.900
A poker game also opens the Next Generation
episode "The Measure of a Man," in which Data's

00:02:47.900 --> 00:02:49.760
humanity is put on trial.

00:02:49.760 --> 00:02:54.560
In that episode, Data also uses poker as an
analogy to try to illustrate the difference

00:02:54.560 --> 00:02:56.560
between knowledge and memories.

00:02:56.560 --> 00:03:01.500
The themes of that episode feel relevant to
Picard a sad future in which synthetics like

00:03:01.500 --> 00:03:06.060
Data have been deemed too dangerous to exist,
and have been outlawed entirely.

00:03:09.240 --> 00:03:14.160
When Jean-Luc is getting ready for his interview,
evaluating his appearance in the mirror, we

00:03:14.160 --> 00:03:18.980
get a brief glimpse of a cream-colored fedora
with a black band hanging on a coat rack behind

00:03:18.980 --> 00:03:19.760
him.

00:03:19.760 --> 00:03:24.700
From the perspective of the camera, the hat
almost appears to be sitting on Picard's head

00:03:24.700 --> 00:03:26.160
at a jaunty angle.

00:03:26.160 --> 00:03:31.180
What at first glance could be mistaken for
just a random piece of set dressing actually

00:03:31.180 --> 00:03:36.730
hearkens back to one of Picard's favorite
holodeck characters the 20th century detective,

00:03:36.730 --> 00:03:37.740
Dixon Hill.

00:03:37.740 --> 00:03:43.000
The gumshoe was one of young Jean-Luc's childhood
heroes, and Picard donned the private investigator's

00:03:43.010 --> 00:03:48.180
characteristic trench coat and fedora on numerous
occasions during The Next Generation's run,

00:03:48.180 --> 00:03:51.620
most notably during the season one episode
"The Big Goodbye."

00:03:51.620 --> 00:03:56.720
In that episode, the simulated mystery Picard
is trying to solve on the holodeck involves

00:03:56.730 --> 00:04:00.860
the death of a young woman, foreshadowing
the real-life puzzle he finds himself caught

00:04:00.860 --> 00:04:02.580
up in as Picard begins.

00:04:03.020 --> 00:04:08.000
During Picard's fateful interview about the
Romulan supernova and his retirement from

00:04:08.010 --> 00:04:13.540
Starfleet, the interviewer mentions that the
Utopia Planitia shipyards on Mars were completely

00:04:13.540 --> 00:04:16.260
destroyed in the synthetic attacks during
the supernova.

00:04:16.260 --> 00:04:21.240
It's a passing comment in a laundry list of
the catastrophic events that brought Picard

00:04:21.240 --> 00:04:23.780
and the Federation to their now-opposing positions.

00:04:23.780 --> 00:04:29.320
But "Utopia Planitia" isn't just some random
futuristic words thrown in for emphasis the

00:04:29.320 --> 00:04:32.900
shipyard has a lot of significance in Star
Trek lore.

00:04:32.900 --> 00:04:37.980
The Utopia Planitia shipyards served as one
of the primary Starfleet ship construction

00:04:37.980 --> 00:04:40.540
sites across several Star Trek series.

00:04:40.540 --> 00:04:46.160
Among the many ships that were constructed
there were the USS Voyager, the USS Defiant

00:04:46.160 --> 00:04:51.080
which featured prominently in Star Trek: Deep
Space Nine and of course, the flagship herself,

00:04:51.080 --> 00:04:53.880
the USS Enterprise 1701-D.

00:04:53.880 --> 00:04:59.220
Further, Commander Benjamin Sisko served for
several years at Utopia Planitia before taking

00:04:59.220 --> 00:05:01.540
command of the Deep Space Nine space station.

00:05:02.160 --> 00:05:07.540
"I would have thought that after three years
spent at the Utopia Planitia yards, that you

00:05:07.540 --> 00:05:09.340
would be ready for a change."

00:05:09.560 --> 00:05:14.560
Jean-Luc has several dreams of Data during
the first episode of Star Trek: Picard, but

00:05:14.570 --> 00:05:18.770
one of the most pivotal moments occurs when
he dreams of Data painting a woman in white

00:05:18.770 --> 00:05:21.320
robes gazing out over a turbulent sea.

00:05:21.320 --> 00:05:27.360
This dream cues Picard to go find a painting
the real Data gifted to him 30 years earlier,

00:05:27.360 --> 00:05:32.280
which to Picard's extreme surprise depicts
the same young woman who showed up in Picard's

00:05:32.280 --> 00:05:34.100
vineyard earlier in the episode.

00:05:34.100 --> 00:05:39.200
While Data explored a lot of artistic pursuits
over the course of The Next Generation, his

00:05:39.200 --> 00:05:44.580
painting in Picard seems to call back specifically
to the sixth-season episode "Birthright, Part

00:05:44.580 --> 00:05:49.420
1," in which Data experiences a confusing
vision and asks Captain Picard how he should

00:05:49.420 --> 00:05:51.060
go about interpreting it.

00:05:51.060 --> 00:05:56.220
Picard urges him to explore what he saw in
a way that feels meaningful to him, and Data

00:05:56.220 --> 00:05:59.540
decides to use painting to explore the images
in his head.

00:05:59.540 --> 00:06:04.419
Ultimately, after painting dozens of pictures,
Data determines that he has accessed a new

00:06:04.420 --> 00:06:09.840
level of his programming and can now dream,
which he resolves to continue doing henceforth.

00:06:09.840 --> 00:06:15.200
It seems only fitting that Data's painting
pursuits would come full circle in Picard,

00:06:15.200 --> 00:06:19.560
with Jean-Luc using the image of Data painting
in his dreams to find meaning in what's happening

00:06:19.560 --> 00:06:20.680
in his real life.

00:06:20.680 --> 00:06:25.720
Even in death, it seems, his old Lieutenant
Commander is still able to help him out.

00:06:28.040 --> 00:06:32.860
When Picard goes to visit his vault in the
Starfleet Archives, we get a brief glimpse

00:06:32.860 --> 00:06:37.320
of two prominently displayed model ships,
neither of which is the Enterprise.

00:06:37.320 --> 00:06:42.780
The first is the USS Stargazer, the first
starship Picard ever commanded.

00:06:42.780 --> 00:06:47.120
This was the ship on which he would first
perform the battle tactic that eventually

00:06:47.130 --> 00:06:52.020
came to be called the "Picard Maneuver," using
a brief burst from the ship's warp drive to

00:06:52.020 --> 00:06:56.330
confuse the sensors of the opposing ship,
making it appear as though the Stargazer was

00:06:56.330 --> 00:06:58.520
in two places at the same time.

00:06:58.520 --> 00:07:03.800
While Picard sometimes spoke disparagingly
of the Stargazer, a ramshackle ship with an

00:07:03.800 --> 00:07:08.200
overworked crew, he did confess to occasionally
longing for the way things used to be.

00:07:08.820 --> 00:07:15.540
"There are times when I would give almost
anything to command the Stargazer again."

00:07:16.060 --> 00:07:21.500
The second model is the captain's yacht from
the Enterprise-E, christened the Cousteau.

00:07:21.500 --> 00:07:27.280
The Cousteau was featured in the third TNG
movie, Star Trek: Insurrection, in which Picard

00:07:27.280 --> 00:07:30.820
and the crew of the Enterprise stood against
the Federation to keep them from forcibly

00:07:30.820 --> 00:07:35.420
relocating a peaceful species so that they
could plunder their planet's resources.

00:07:35.420 --> 00:07:41.160
In Insurrection, Picard temporarily resigns
from Starfleet, disgusted by their abandoning

00:07:41.169 --> 00:07:44.990
of the Prime Directive and their dismissal
of innocent lives in the name of their own

00:07:44.990 --> 00:07:50.130
self-interest which is exactly the same issue
that ultimately led to his permanent resignation

00:07:50.130 --> 00:07:52.260
following the Romulan supernova.

00:07:52.560 --> 00:07:57.480
There is also a Klingon weapon in Picard's
vault in the Starfleet Archives, a curved

00:07:57.480 --> 00:07:59.320
blade called a bat'leth.

00:07:59.320 --> 00:08:03.920
The Next Generation often featured Lieutenant
Commander Worf training and fighting with

00:08:03.920 --> 00:08:07.100
a bat'leth that had been in his family for
generations.

00:08:07.100 --> 00:08:12.460
It's unclear exactly where Picard's bat'leth
came from; while it's possible it's Worf's,

00:08:12.460 --> 00:08:16.560
it could also be one that Picard came by through
his other dealings with the Klingons.

00:08:16.560 --> 00:08:21.680
Picard notably served as Arbiter of Succession
at the request of the Klingon leader K'mpec

00:08:21.690 --> 00:08:26.270
during the Next Generation episodes "Reunion"
and "Redemption," and it's possible his high

00:08:26.270 --> 00:08:31.080
standing with the Klingon Empire led at some
point to him receiving his own bat'leth.

00:08:31.080 --> 00:08:35.840
Regardless of where Picard's bat'leth came
from, the weapon's presence in his vault serves

00:08:35.849 --> 00:08:40.659
as a reminder that Picard is highly respected
among the Klingons, who used to be the Federation's

00:08:40.660 --> 00:08:41.980
fiercest enemies.

00:08:41.980 --> 00:08:47.040
That theme feels significant to Star Trek:
Picard, which reveals that the former officer

00:08:47.040 --> 00:08:51.500
resigned from Starfleet over their treatment
of the Romulans, who stepped into the role

00:08:51.500 --> 00:08:55.840
of primary Trek villains after the Federation
finally made peace with the Klingons.

00:08:56.140 --> 00:09:00.840
One of the final treasured items that Jean-Luc
Picard has kept stored away in his vault all

00:09:00.850 --> 00:09:05.029
these years is the banner that the children
of the Enterprise made for him to celebrate

00:09:05.029 --> 00:09:09.619
"Captain Picard Day," an annual event in which
the children of the starship honor its captain

00:09:09.620 --> 00:09:11.160
through original artwork.

00:09:11.160 --> 00:09:16.060
While Picard was reluctant to participate
in Captain Picard Day while aboard the Enterprise,

00:09:16.069 --> 00:09:20.430
it would seem that he may have appreciated
it more than he let on, if he kept the banner

00:09:20.430 --> 00:09:22.600
for decades after leaving his post.

00:09:22.600 --> 00:09:28.080
We witness Picard receiving the banner in
The Next Generation's season 7 episode "The

00:09:28.089 --> 00:09:32.240
Pegasus," which sees the crew of the Enterprise
facing off against the Romulans to recover

00:09:32.240 --> 00:09:34.480
a Starfleet ship near the neutral zone.

00:09:34.480 --> 00:09:38.940
During the course of the episode, it becomes
clear that Starfleet violated their peace

00:09:38.949 --> 00:09:43.500
treaty with the Romulans and tried to cover
it up, but Picard refuses to go along with

00:09:43.500 --> 00:09:48.040
the deception, revealing the truth to the
Romulans and siding against his Starfleet

00:09:48.040 --> 00:09:48.920
colleagues.

00:09:48.920 --> 00:09:53.680
In short, this is yet another Easter egg in
Picard's first episode that calls back to

00:09:53.680 --> 00:09:58.470
a time when our hero took a stand against
the Federation, possibly foreshadowing that

00:09:58.470 --> 00:10:03.440
the one-time captain and Starfleet are about
to find themselves at odds once more.

00:10:05.020 --> 00:10:10.519
When Picard travels to the Daystrom Institute
in Okinawa in order to dig into the possibility

00:10:10.519 --> 00:10:16.249
that Dahj may be a flesh and blood synthetic,
he meets with Dr. Agnes Jurati, who assures

00:10:16.249 --> 00:10:21.180
him that sentient flesh and blood synthetics
are nothing more than a forgotten pipe dream.

00:10:21.180 --> 00:10:25.760
Jurati then mentions that she was recruited
by Dr. Bruce Maddox, and that the two of them

00:10:25.760 --> 00:10:29.740
had worked together to create a sentient android
similar to Data.

00:10:29.740 --> 00:10:34.360
She says they'd been close until the synthetic
ban, after which Maddox disappeared.

00:10:34.360 --> 00:10:38.500
If the name of the missing scientist sounds
familiar, there's a good reason for that.

00:10:38.500 --> 00:10:43.639
Bruce Maddox first appeared in the Next Generation
season 2 episode "The Measure of a Man," in

00:10:43.639 --> 00:10:48.179
which he argued against Data's sentience in
the hopes of being able to override his free

00:10:48.180 --> 00:10:49.700
will and study him.

00:10:49.700 --> 00:10:55.240
Opposing Maddox was none other than Captain
Jean-Luc Picard, who presented a moving and

00:10:55.240 --> 00:10:58.300
ultimately, victorious case for Data's personhood.

00:10:58.300 --> 00:11:03.920
Mentioning Maddox in an episode already rife
with themes of sentience, identity, free will,

00:11:03.920 --> 00:11:08.889
and individuality feels like a very deliberate
nod to one of The Next Generation's most fascinating

00:11:08.889 --> 00:11:13.000
core debates, and hints that we haven't heard
the last of either side of this argument.

00:11:13.220 --> 00:11:18.240
When Picard is telling Dahj his theory about
her origins, she tells Picard about a hybrid

00:11:18.249 --> 00:11:23.740
orchid that her father engineered, splicing
two genuses and naming "the offspring" after

00:11:23.740 --> 00:11:24.340
her.

00:11:24.340 --> 00:11:28.760
Later, Picard mentions to Dr. Jurati that
Data always wanted a daughter.

00:11:28.760 --> 00:11:33.860
Both lines together seem to be a direct reference
to "The Offspring," a season 3 episode of

00:11:33.870 --> 00:11:38.529
The Next Generation in which Data constructs
an android daughter named Lal from his own

00:11:38.529 --> 00:11:39.220
design.

00:11:39.220 --> 00:11:44.240
In "The Offspring," Lal evolves quickly, surpassing
even Data's abilities in a short amount of

00:11:44.249 --> 00:11:49.990
time and revealing that she can feel emotion,
but ultimately her experiences wind up overloading

00:11:49.990 --> 00:11:51.500
her positronic brain.

00:11:51.500 --> 00:11:55.060
Despite Data's best efforts, he is unable
to save her.

00:11:55.060 --> 00:11:59.720
Data then downloads Lal's memories into his
own neural net, so that in a way, she can

00:11:59.720 --> 00:12:01.009
live on through him.

00:12:01.009 --> 00:12:06.009
"The Offspring" also sees Picard side against
the Federation once again, when a Starfleet

00:12:06.009 --> 00:12:11.140
Admiral attempts to claim Lal for scientific
research, and Picard refuses to let her go.

00:12:11.140 --> 00:12:16.720
Dahj's journey in Picard's first episode mirrors
Lal's in many ways in how Picard tried to

00:12:16.720 --> 00:12:21.760
protect her, and ultimately, in how she died
after only being truly awake for a brief time.

